# fixedbugs, M-separate-compilation, 2026-08-25 (panel 092's queued half).
#
# SYMPTOM: `extern function pow(x: f32, y: f64) -> f64` compiled clean, ran,
# and printed 13780.642208527162 where C's own answer is 13780.612339822381 —
# wrong at the fifth digit, exit 0. The sitting measured it and queued the
# repair; measuring the repair found three MORE silent directions beside it:
# `f64` where the header says `float` (rounded), a float where the header
# takes an integer (the fraction dropped), and an integer where the header
# holds a float (large values rounded). All four at exit 0, under the
# project's exact flags, on the day the sitting closed.
#
# CAUSE: every one of these conversions is legal C, and none of the flags the
# compiler passes globally reads it. The probe — the never-called function
# whose call makes clang judge each parameter — was compiled under the same
# silence.
#
# FIX: the probe block is wrapped in `#pragma clang diagnostic error` for
# four names — double-promotion, implicit-float-conversion, float-conversion,
# implicit-int-conversion — each with a measured witness, and only inside the
# block: a probe's arguments are exactly the declared types, so an implicit
# conversion THERE is a declaration disagreeing with the header, while the
# generated code outside converts through deliberate casts no implicit-
# conversion warning reads. What stays silent is what C converts without
# changing any value, plus what a pointer points at — the spec names both.
#
# This file must NOT compile. It is built by the CLI surface test
# `a float parameter never converts in silence` (suite_surface).

extern "math.h"
    function pow(x: f32, y: f64) -> f64  #~ ffi_parameter_type
    function powf(x: f64, y: f32) -> f32  #~ ffi_parameter_type
    function ldexp(v: f64, n: f32) -> f64  #~ ffi_parameter_type
    function sqrt(x: i64) -> f64  #~ ffi_parameter_type

function main()
    print(pow(x: to_f32(1.234), y: 45.3))
    print(to_f64(powf(x: 2.0, y: to_f32(10.0))))
    print(ldexp(v: 1.5, n: to_f32(3.0)))
    print(sqrt(x: 2))
